    <header class="page-hero">
      <div class="wrap">
        <p class="kicker">Figuring out what you want</p>
        <h1>The property is the last question, not the first.</h1>
        <p class="lede">People write me a neighborhood and a budget. The useful conversation starts earlier: how many months you will actually be here, what your body does in heat and altitude, and whether a cheap lot still looks cheap after a roof, a tank, and a road.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="btn" href="quiz.html">Try the region quiz</a>
          <a class="btn secondary" href="contact.html">Send the intake</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap measure">
        <h2>What I ask on an intake</h2>
        <p>The <a href="contact.html">contact form</a> is this list. Answer what you know. “Not sure” is a real answer.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Budget, and whether that number is the land only or the land plus a building.</li>
          <li>How many months a year you expect to be in Peru.</li>
          <li>Retirement, a vacation base, a rental, or a purchase you might sell. More than one can be true. None of them is a promise of profit.</li>
          <li>Whether you intend to rent the place out, short stays or long.</li>
          <li>Tolerance for heat, humidity, altitude, rain, noise, and isolation.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>The life around the house</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>How close you need a real hospital, not a pharmacy with a red cross.</li>
          <li>Airports: an international hub, a domestic flight, or a long road.</li>
          <li>International schools, if children are part of the plan.</li>
          <li>An expat scene, full immersion, or something in between.</li>
          <li>Spanish: little, daily-life, or comfortable. Little Spanish is workable in parts of Lima and in a few beach towns. It is a hard way to buy land.</li>
          <li>Internet for remote work. Fiber in town is a different fact from a rural tower or a satellite dish.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>Altitude, before you fall in love with a photo</h2>
        <p class="measure">Many people do not think about this until the second night. I am not a doctor. If you have heart, lung, blood, or pregnancy concerns, talk to your own clinician before you plan a life above 2,000 meters.</p>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Cusco</h3>
            <p>The historic center sits at about 3,400 meters. Headache, bad sleep, and shortness of breath are ordinary for new arrivals. A three-day tour is not a test of a year.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Arequipa</h3>
            <p>About 2,300 meters. Lower than Cusco, dry, and still high enough that some people feel it, especially with a heart or lung condition.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Huancayo</h3>
            <p>About 3,200 meters, in the central highlands. The city is practical and commercial. The altitude is not a footnote, and the road to Lima can close in the rains.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Puno</h3>
            <p>About 3,800 meters, on Lake Titicaca. This is among the highest cities buyers treat as a normal option. Cold is part of the day, not a surprise season.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p class="measure">The Sacred Valley is lower than Cusco’s plaza, often around 2,800 meters depending on the town. Lower is not the same as easy. Read the <a href="journal/altitude.html">altitude essay</a> and the <a href="places.html">region notes</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap measure">
        <h2>What “cheap property in Peru” looks like</h2>
        <p>A low number usually buys land, or an unfinished shell, or a house that still needs the unglamorous half of a house. Brick walls with no windows. A roof that leaks at the joint. Wiring that a cousin added. No registered building, only the land on the title. A lot that is cheap because the road dies in the rain, or because the seller cannot show a registered owner.</p>
        <p>Common disappointments, in plain order:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>The photo was taken at noon on a Saturday. Friday night is a <span lang="es">discoteca</span>, a church loudspeaker, or roosters.</li>
          <li>The price was set for a foreign buyer. Local asking prices, in Spanish, tell a different story. I call that gringo pricing, and I compare against ranges I know.</li>
          <li>The “house” is <span lang="es">autoconstrucción</span>: built without an engineer, in a country that has earthquakes.</li>
          <li>The paper is a <span lang="es">constancia de posesión</span>, not a registered title.</li>
          <li>The beach lot is beautiful and may sit inside the 50 km border strip, where foreigners generally cannot own without special authorization.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Building, renovating, and maintaining cost in kind, not in a number I am willing to print as fact. Labor from a <span lang="es">maestro de obra</span> is often the smaller surprise. Cement, steel, tile, a real electrical install, a cistern, and a roof that survives a hard rain are the larger ones. Jungle buildings also fight humidity and termites. A simple extra room can be a modest local project. A finished house with reliable water and decent wiring is a different project. Get written quotes for the specific plan. Treat any dollar figure in a Facebook group as a rumor.</p>
        <p>More on this in <a href="journal/cheap.html">what cheap property looks like</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="wrap note">
        <h2>Rent in the area before you buy</h2>
        <p>Many people should live in a place for a few months before they own anything there. January and July are different countries in the same valley: rain, fog, tourists, empty streets, water pressure. A rental shows you the hospital road, the market, and whether you sleep.</p>
        <p>I can help arrange that. It is a smaller piece of work than a purchase, and it is often the one that saves the purchase. Say so on the <a href="contact.html">intake</a> if you want a rental first.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
